      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes a lot of sense. The distinction between a common pattern and a unique, proprietary one seems to be the crux of the issue.\n\nTo follow up on that: if a dispute did arise, what kind of evidence or \"paper trail\" would be most effective for a consultant to show they were using general knowledge rather than stolen IP? For instance, if I can show that I applied similar architectural principles in much earlier, unrelated projects, would that help prove the logic was part of my \"unaided memory\" and general expertise rather than something derived specifically from this client? I'm trying to figure out how to build a professional defense in case they get aggressive about it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
