      "content": "If a dispute arises, having a well-documented \"paper trail\" can be crucial in demonstrating that you used general knowledge and expertise rather than stolen intellectual property (IP). Here are some types of evidence that could support your position:\n\n1. **Development Documentation**: Keep detailed records of your development process, including design documents, architecture diagrams, and code comments that show your thought process and how you arrived at the solution independently.\n\n2. **Version Control History**: Use version control systems like Git to track changes and demonstrate the evolution of your work. This can show that your implementation was developed over time and not copied from the client's project.\n\n3. **Prior Art**: Present examples of similar architectural principles or patterns from earlier, unrelated projects. This can help establish that the logic you used is part of your general expertise and not unique to the client's project.\n\n4. **Expert Testimony**: In some cases, it may be beneficial to have an expert in the field review your work and provide testimony that the logic pattern is common knowledge within the industry.\n\n5. **Independent Creation Evidence**: Show that you had the opportunity to learn and develop the logic pattern independently, such as through industry publications, conferences, or courses you attended.\n\n6.",
